    "applies_only_to_state_regulated_plans": "State insurance law generally does not reach self-funded (ERISA) employer plans, which cover roughly two-thirds of workers with employer coverage. Every state protection below is gated on the reader's plan being fully insured.",
    "built": "2026-09-07",
    "known_gaps": [
      "Ground-ambulance protections are now recorded for all 51 jurisdictions from the Commonwealth Fund / Georgetown CHIR map, updated February 2026. Its map data names 22 states with protections; the surrounding prose on the same page says 24, and that discrepancy is unresolved -- the 22 the data actually names are what is recorded here. Every one of these laws reaches fully insured plans only.",
      "Several of these statutes SUNSET: Texas 1 Sep 2027, Utah 1 Jul 2027, Mississippi 30 Jun 2028, and Washington's 325%-of-Medicare fallback 31 Dec 2027. Illinois expands to private state-regulated plans on 1 Jan 2027. Re-verify before those dates.",
      "Charity-care thresholds, state assistance programmes and surprise-billing laws are recorded only for the 24 states researched in the original pass. The 27 states added from the ambulance map carry null for those fields, which means NOT RESEARCHED.",
      "Statutes of limitations, medical-debt credit-reporting rules and wage-garnishment limits remain largely unmodelled: debt_sol_years is set for one state."
    ],
    "note": "HAND-CURATED. Unlike the other files in this directory, this one is NOT produced by tools/build_data.py from a federal source. Every entry is researched by hand and must carry citations and a `verified` date. Absence of a state means NOT RESEARCHED -- which is never the same as 'no protection'. Do not fill a field with false to make a map look complete.",
